= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

For a <continuously differentiable vector field> $\mathbf F$ on a neighborhood of the closure of $V$, the <divergence theorem> states
$$
\boxed{\iiint_V\nabla\cdot\mathbf F\,dV=\iint_{\partial V}\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf n\,dS.}
$$
Here $\mathbf n$ is the outward <unit normal vector>; the right side is the outward <flux> through the <boundary>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Apply the <product rule> to each <vector component> and sum:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\nabla\cdot(\phi\mathbf F)
&=\sum_{j=1}^3\partial_{x_j}(\phi F_j)\\
&=\phi\sum_{j=1}^3\partial_{x_j}F_j+\sum_{j=1}^3(\partial_{x_j}\phi)F_j\\
&=\boxed{\phi\nabla\cdot\mathbf F+\nabla\phi\cdot\mathbf F.}
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus the <divergence> of a <scalar field> times a <vector field> contains a <divergence> term and a <dot product> with the <gradient> of the <scalar field>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Choose $\mathbf F=\nabla\psi$ in the preceding <product rule> and apply the <divergence theorem>. Since $\nabla\cdot\nabla\psi=\Delta\psi$, this gives the first of <Green's identities>:
$$
\boxed{\iiint_V\bigl(\phi\Delta\psi+\nabla\phi\cdot\nabla\psi\bigr),dV
=\iint_{\partial V}\phi\,\partial_n\psi\,dS.}
$$
Here $\partial_n\psi=\mathbf n\cdot\nabla\psi$ is the outward <normal derivative>. Interchange $\phi$ and $\psi$, then subtract. The <gradient> <dot products> cancel, yielding the second of <Green's identities>:
$$
\boxed{\iiint_V(\phi\Delta\psi-\psi\Delta\phi)\,dV
=\iint_{\partial V}(\phi\partial_n\psi-\psi\partial_n\phi)\,dS.}
$$
For example, $\phi,\psi\in C^2(\overline V)$ with the needed extensions suffice for these applications of the <divergence theorem>.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

**False.** The <constant function> $u=1$ is a <harmonic function> and has zero <normal derivative> on every smooth <boundary>, but it is nonzero in $V$.

In fact, the first of <Green's identities> gives
$$
\iiint_V|\nabla u|^2\,dV=\iint_{\partial V}u\partial_nu\,dS-\iiint_Vu\Delta u\,dV=0.
$$
Hence $\nabla u=0$; <path connectedness> makes $u$ constant throughout $V$. Thus this homogeneous <Neumann boundary condition> determines a <harmonic function> only up to an additive constant.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

**True.** The first of <Green's identities> and the <Robin boundary condition> $\partial_nu=-u$ give
$$
\iiint_V|\nabla u|^2\,dV=-\iint_{\partial V}u^2\,dS,
\qquad
\boxed{\iiint_V|\nabla u|^2\,dV+\iint_{\partial V}u^2\,dS=0.}
$$
Both <integrands> are nonnegative, so $\nabla u=0$ in $V$ and $u=0$ on the <boundary>. By <path connectedness>, $u$ is a <constant function>, and its <boundary value> makes that constant zero. Therefore $\boxed{u=0}$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

**False.** Take $V=\{x^2+y^2+z^2<1\}$, the open <unit ball>, and $u(x,y,z)=x$. This is a nonzero <harmonic function>. On the <unit sphere>, the outward <unit normal vector> is $\mathbf n=(x,y,z)$, so
$$
\partial_nu=\mathbf n\cdot(1,0,0)=x=u.
$$
Thus the prescribed <Robin boundary condition> holds. Unlike the preceding case, <Green's identities> now give $\int_V|\nabla u|^2=\int_{\partial V}u^2$, which permits nonzero solutions of the <differential equation>.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

**True.** Two applications of <Green's identities>, or two <integrations by parts>, give
$$
\iiint_Vu\Delta^2u\,dV
=\iiint_V(\Delta u)^2\,dV
+\iint_{\partial V}\bigl(u\partial_n\Delta u-\Delta u\partial_nu\bigr)\,dS.
$$
The <biharmonic equation> makes the left side zero, while the two <clamped boundary conditions> $u=\partial_nu=0$ annihilate the <boundary integral>. Consequently
$$
\iiint_V(\Delta u)^2\,dV=0,
$$
so $\Delta u=0$. Apply the first of <Green's identities> once more with $\phi=\psi=u$: because $u=0$ on the <boundary>, it gives $\int_V|\nabla u|^2=0$. By <path connectedness>, $u$ is constant, and the zero <boundary value> implies $\boxed{u=0}$. This argument assumes the classical differentiability needed for the displayed <integrations by parts>.

